  "founding_year": 1983,
  "history": "Battery Ventures was founded in Boston in 1983 by Rick Frisbie, Howard Anderson and Bob Barrett, and is one of the two oldest firms in this project's book. Its first two decades were spent in enterprise systems, networking and communications \u2014 Cullinet, Legent, Mentor Graphics, Banyan Systems, FORE Systems, Xircom, Akamai and the wireless roll-ups that became Nextel \u2014 and its published record still carries that 1980s and 1990s book in full. The firm now invests from Boston, San Francisco, New York, Menlo Park, London and Tel Aviv under a single adviser, Battery Management Corp. (CRD 160921), with the UK and Israel offices operating as relying advisers. What makes Battery unusual is that one fund family does all of it: the same flagship writes seed cheques, growth rounds and control buyouts, which is why this base publishes only the minority venture slice of a firm whose own investment list is larger.",
  "investment_concept": "One fund, the whole ladder. Battery raises a flagship and a paired side fund each vintage \u2014 XIV at $3.5B in 2022, XV at $3.25B in 2026 \u2014 plus a later-stage Select sleeve, and invests from seed through growth and into control buyouts out of that one pool rather than through separate vehicles. The published sector shape follows: application and infrastructure software first, then consumer, industrial technology and life-science tools. Israel is a genuine second home, at 8% of the base.",
